  week from now and we all understand that  so i don't want to use the words of gold  star families as some political punching  bag but i just was i hadn't had the  wherewithal  as the president united states to be  there be in the moment and show them  that respect and if they say he didn't  i'll take the word for it and just say  that we all deserve better  let me ask you sam brown you graduated  from west point you got a great  distinction you became an airborne  ranger september 08 you were struck by  an ied it severely wounded you a  three-year journey of rehabilitation and  recovery  um your thoughts today  do you have any regrets about serving  the way this now has ended  you know sean i've got a message for the  american people and this is not just me  this is after talking to many other  veterans who served there  some my own family members i'm i'm the  one of three in my direct family who  have served in afghanistan and there's  there's two themes that's coming out  from this  one is most of us don't regret what we  did there because at the end of the day  we are confident in the help and the  assistance and the security that we  provided for the afghans that we had  care over at that time and for whatever  period of time that they had some some  shelter and security from the taliban or  other threats that was worth something  and we're we we feel good about that but  what we don't appreciate and what joe  biden has almost single-handedly along  with the the top brass leadership out of  dod has done has caused many of us  to feel that we have been lied to over  the years about our commitment to the  afghan people into the mission there and  here's here's the really sad thing and  here's what america needs to know  is and sean i know you know this  many of those who serve in uniform come  from multi-generational families  here's here's the second thing that  people are telling me today  we're done  joe biden has lost the trust and faith  of those families  